{
 "note": "Hamilton Drive on the 1901 census - thirteen households, seventy-six people - read from the enumerator's page and checked line by line by A. Hagues, who supplied the head of the first household after my transcription of that page turned out to begin part way down it.\n\n**The record holds nothing at all for Hamilton Drive in 1901**, and only 1921 besides.\n\n**Every household here is unfiled, and that is deliberate.** The record keeps sixteen properties on this street, numbered 1 to 11 with 1a, 1b and 1c, then 24, then Ferndene. The enumerator kept names: **Fairfield**, **Oakleigh**, **Glendale**, **Beechwood**, **Douglas House**. Not one of those names appears against any property, on this street or any other, so there is nothing to file them to. See [[1901-house-names-not-in-the-record]].\n\n**Two households are large and worth finding houses for.** Oakleigh holds nine under Ralph J. Horner, professor of music, with a son who is also a professor of music and a nephew returned as a Canadian cattle rancher. Beechwood holds nine, mostly boarders - a civil engineer, two lace warehousemen, a Swiss chemical manufacturer, a commercial traveller from Curacao and a professional sick nurse.\n\n**One household was mended by A. Hagues.** Schedule 74 had only three people in my reading, the page having begun mid-household; he supplied its head, John A Scampton, a yarn agent of 26, and his sister Annie.\n\nWhere the enumerator headed a schedule with a number alone, the household is set down against the street rather than left with no address at all.",
 "source": "1901 census enumerator's page for Hamilton Drive, Nottingham, transcribed by A. Hagues.",
